well we're at a hospital on the eastern  outskirts of kathmandu and this is a  place i think which really illustrates  the multiple challenges  facing the people of nepal as you can  see we're under tarpaulin there is a  fully functioning hospital here  it's not damaged structural engineers  have looked at it but despite that  people are simply unwilling to go inside  because they're so scared that there  might be more aftershocks you can see  more patients  coming in here  they're young they're old we've seen a  huge number of children and i think for  them it is the most difficult i suppose  the positive news from this hospital at  least is that none of the patients are  critical the doctor who we spoke to  earlier on said that they are all stable  they will all recover certainly from  their physical injuries but it's the  traumatic  psychological injuries if you like which  i think will take much much longer for  them to recover from  we've moved outside the hospital grounds  now and many more tents here what  they've done is that they've effectively  moved every ward from the hospital  outside they've labeled the wards up so  we've got ward number one two three all  the way up to ten uh and the beds have  come outside as well and it's  challenging because the monsoon season  is coming there were some pretty large  black clouds earlier on if it rains it's  going to become  that much more miserable  you can see some of the injuries here  many people have got leg injuries this  is simply from falling masonry they're  not  very badly injured but they need  treatment they're getting treatment  local doctors here we've seen uh some  military from the canadian military as  well they've been erecting some of these  tents unicef is here as well so the aid  is getting through but  the challenges are so tricky because  people simply don't want to go inside  because they're so frightened that there  will be another aftershock of course  that one  on tuesday was huge but overnight  tuesday into wednesday there were many  more aftershocks i was speaking to the  doctor earlier and he said that during  one of the big aftershocks there was an  operation going on so while those people  who were  able to move outside the hospital did  one of the patients was under an  anesthetic the doctor carried on  treating him carried on with the  operation despite the ground moving  underneath them and only then did they  move outside a very very difficult  time for the people of nepal
 

